    "q-221394": {
      "dom_id": "step-q221394",
      "name": "Question 2",
      "problem": "Let $q$ be a prime number. Which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. If ${5}^{3}\\,|\\text{ }{q}^{6}$ then $q=5$\n\nII. If ${q}^{3}\\,|\\,{4}^{2}$ then $q=4$\n\nIII. If ${q}^{3}\\,|\\,{4}^{2}$ then $q=2$",
      "worked_solution": "For any integers $m$ and $n$ and any prime numbers $p$ and $q,$ we have that\n\n${p}^{m}∣{q}^{n}\\,\\text{or}\\,{q}^{n}∣{p}^{m}\\,⟹\\,p=q.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each of the statements.\n\n- Statement I is true. As $5$ and $q$ are prime numbers, $3$ and $6$ are integers, and ${5}^{3}\\,|\\text{ }{q}^{6},$ we must have $q=5.$\n\n- Statement II is false. As a counterexample, let $q=2.$ Then, ${2}^{3}∣{4}^{2}$ but $2\\ne 4.$\n\n- Statement III is true. Since ${4}^{2}={2}^{4}$ and ${q}^{3}\\,|\\,{4}^{2},$ then ${q}^{3}\\,|\\,{2}^{4}.$ As $q$ and $2$ are prime numbers, and ${q}^{3}\\,|\\text{ }{2}^{4},$ we must have $q=2.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\"",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
      "fields": [
        {
          "key": "selection",
          "type": "radio",
