    "q-221390": {
      "dom_id": "step-q221390",
      "name": "Question 3",
      "problem": "Let $p$ be a prime number. Which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. If $3∣p$ then $p=3$\n\nII. If $p\\,|\\,9$ then $p=9$\n\nIII. If $p\\,|\\,15$ then $p=3$",
      "worked_solution": "For any prime numbers $p$ and $q,$\n\n$p∣q\\,\\text{or}\\,q∣p\\,⟹\\,p=q.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each of the statements.\n\n- Statement I is true. As $3$ and $p$ are prime numbers and $3∣p,$ we must have $p=3.$\n\n- Statement II is false. As a counterexample, let $p=3.$ Then, $3∣9$ but $3\\ne 9.$\n\n- Statement III is false. As a counterexample, let $p=5.$ Then, $5∣15$ but $5\\ne 3.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
