    "q-93635": {
      "dom_id": "step-q93635",
      "name": "Question 7",
      "problem": "Which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. If $3\\,|\\,6a$ then $3\\,|\\,a$\n\nII. If $7\\,|\\,5a$ then $7\\,|\\,a$\n\nIII. If $4\\,|\\,2a$ then $4\\,|\\,a$",
      "worked_solution": "For any integers $a$ and $b,$ and any prime $p,$\n\n$p\\,|\\,ab$ $\\,⟹\\,$ $p\\,|\\,a\\,$ or $\\,p\\,|\\,b.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each of the statements.\n\n- Statement I is false. As a counterexample, let $a=2.$ Then, $3∣6\\cdot 2$ but $3∤2.$\n\n- Statement II is true.  As $7$ is prime and $7\\,|\\,5a,$ we must have $7\\,|\\,5$ or $7\\,|\\,a.$ Since $5$ is not divisible by $7,$ we obtain that $a$ must be divisible by $7.$\n\n- Statement III is false. As a counterexample, let $a=2.$ Then, $4∣2\\cdot 2$ but $4∤2.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\"",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
